# Test-Driven Development

**`main` (mod) policy.** Base-game branch `master` has no unit-test suite. All new mod work on `main` requires TDD for production behavior.

## Iron Law

No production behavior change without a failing test first. If a change landed before its test, delete the change and start over.

## Workflow

### 1. Lock in current logic (if untested)

Before changing behavior, check for coverage of the logic you will touch.

- If tests are missing, **write characterization tests for the current behavior first**.
- Those tests should **pass against today's code** (document what it does now).
- Do not change production code in this step.

### 2. Red → Implement → Green (for new or changed behavior)

1. **RED** — One minimal test for the desired new/changed behavior. `@DisplayName` + AssertJ (`Assertions.assertThat`).
2. **Verify RED** — Fails for missing behavior, not a typo/setup error.
3. **IMPLEMENT** — Smallest production change that passes.
4. **Verify GREEN** — Re-run; all must pass (including characterization tests from step 1).
5. **REFACTOR** — Clean up only after green; no new behavior.

```bash
# Always quiet + skip Error Prone flood (see java-quality-checks). Windows: gradlew.bat
./gradlew :core:test -q -PerrorProneOff --tests "com.shatteredpixel.shatteredpixeldungeon.heroechoes.EchoAndStorageTest"
./gradlew :core:test -q -PerrorProneOff --tests "com.shatteredpixel.shatteredpixeldungeon.heroechoes.EchoAndStorageTest.fromHeroCapturesRequiredFields"
```

**Agent test runs:** always pass `-q -PerrorProneOff`. Do not dump EP / lifecycle noise into the session. Drop those flags only when the user asks for Error Prone output or a PR quality gate.

## Test quality — assert behavior, not execution

A test earns its place by failing when the **game is wrong**, not when the code path is
merely absent. Most weak tests here pass because something happened at all.

### Assert the value, not the direction

Exact numbers are the point for anything the player feels: charge cost, cooldown, damage,
HP scaling per depth, spawn charges, durations, drop counts.

```java
// ❌ WEAK — passes if the cost changes from 35 to 1
Assertions.assertThat(armor.charge).isLessThan(chargeBefore);
// ✅ STRONG — a balance change must update this test deliberately
Assertions.assertThat(armor.charge).isEqualTo(chargeBefore - 35);
```

Use `isLessThan` / `isGreaterThan` only when the exact value is genuinely
non-deterministic (RNG rolls), and then bound both ends.

### Assert the magnitude, not the existence

`buff(Drowsy.class) != null` says a buff attached. Assert the buff's duration, stacks, or
resulting HP/state — the thing that decides the fight.

### Prefer one invariant over N copies

Before writing the 12th near-identical `Echo*KindsTest`, ask whether the property holds for
*every* member of the family, and write that instead:

- "every arsenal item an Echo can use produces the same observable effect as the Hero using it"
- "no armor-ability adapter call leaves the boss unable to act"
- "every `Bundlable` mod class round-trips through save/load unchanged"

Parameterized (`@ParameterizedTest` + `@MethodSource` over the real registry) beats a
hand-maintained file per case: it covers the members nobody remembered to write, and it
fails when someone adds a new one.

Corollary: **crash-safety belongs in a fixture, not in every test.** Sprite-less NPE guards
and "can still take its next turn" checks go in shared `@AfterEach` / `EchoTestSupport`
assertions once, not copy-pasted per ability. Matches
[no-pass-through-wrappers.mdc](iatdb/.cursor/rules/no-pass-through-wrappers.mdc)'s bias:
enforce structurally, don't repeat per site.

### Test our logic, not upstream's

Running a vanilla scroll through an adapter and asserting the vanilla effect re-tests
Shattered Pixel Dungeon. The mod's contract is the *equivalence*: an Echo using item X gets
the same outcome the Hero would. Assert that equivalence, not the effect itself.

### Cover the contract, not the surface

For Hero Echoes the contract is "another player's hero fights as a boss, faithfully, fairly,
reproducibly". So the tests that matter most:

- **Fidelity** — hydrated boss has the same effective stats/kit as the source hero (stronger than a bundle round-trip).
- **Hostile input** — malformed or tampered snapshots from the backend are *rejected*, never clamped into a playable boss (see [fail-dont-fake.mdc](workspace-rules/.cursor/rules/fail-dont-fake.mdc)).
- **Decision quality** — the AI *chooses* correctly (drinks the healing potion at low HP), not merely that it *may* act.
- **Persistence** — save/load across a rename or field addition still yields the same fight.

### Mechanics

- One behavior per test; split if the name needs "and".
- Prefer **real fixtures** over mocking the game (`GdxTestExtension`, `EchoTestSupport`). Test seams (`set...ForTests`) are fine.
- Mirror production packages under `core/src/test/java/`.
- Never fix a bug without a failing reproduction test first.
- EchoBoss / world-VFX combat: use skill `echo-combat-vfx-test` / `EchoTestSupport.installEchoBossLevel` — hollow sprite-less fights hide NPEs.

## Mutation check (the RED gate)

Before accepting a green test, confirm it would catch a real regression: change the
production constant or flip the branch, and the test must fail. If tweaking a number leaves
it green, the assertion is too loose — tighten it. This is what "Verify RED" means for
characterization tests, which have no natural red phase.

## Good vs Bad

```java
// GOOD — exact, one behavior, fails on a balance change
@Test
@DisplayName("HeroicLeap costs 35 armor charge for an Echo")
void leapCostsThirtyFive() {
	WarriorArmor armor = chargedArmor(100);
	EchoArmorAbilityAdapter.activate(boss, armor, new HeroicLeap(), dest);
	Assertions.assertThat(armor.charge).isEqualTo(65f);
}

// GOOD — invariant over the whole family
@ParameterizedTest
@MethodSource("everyArmorAbility")
@DisplayName("every armor ability leaves the Echo boss able to act")
void abilitiesLeaveBossActionable(ArmorAbility ability) { ... }

// BAD — vague, asserts almost nothing
@Test
void testSnapshot() {
	Assertions.assertThat(Echo.create(5, ...)).isNotNull();
}

// BAD — directional; survives cost dropping to zero
@Test
void spendsCharge() {
	Assertions.assertThat(armor.charge).isLessThan(before);
}
```

## Done checklist

- [ ] Untested current logic was characterized with passing tests before changes
- [ ] New/changed behavior had a failing test before implementation
- [ ] Minimal green; `./gradlew :core:test -q -PerrorProneOff` clean
- [ ] Assertions are exact (not merely directional) for every player-facing number
- [ ] Mutation check: perturbing the production value/branch makes the new tests fail
- [ ] A family-wide invariant was considered before adding another per-case test file
- [ ] Feature work: report **tests created**, **gaps**, **redundant** (`@DisplayName` titles); ask before filling/removing

## Exceptions (ask if unsure)

Throwaway prototypes, generated code, config-only (rules/AGENTS/ignore), assets, i18n `.properties`, version/changelog bumps, pure renames with existing coverage.
